Size: pack the engine's relative relocations, for any glibc.

The engine is a position-independent executable with 272 thousand relative
relocations, which take 6.2 MiB as RELA entries of 24 bytes each. lld can
pack them as DT_RELR, about 100 KiB, but only glibc 2.36 and later apply
DT_RELR, and they refuse it without a GLIBC_ABI_DT_RELR version that older
glibc does not have. zebkit runs on glibc 2.17 and later.

So the engine applies them itself. //zebkit/relr adds a function that runs
from .preinit_array, after the loader's own relocation and before any
library constructor (those can call the engine's malloc). In a shared
library (:relr_shared, for libzebkit.so.1) it is the library's DT_INIT
function: the loader calls DT_INIT at the library's address, which needs
no relocation, before it reads .init_array, whose entries are packed
relocations themselves. The link step, the executable's or the library's,
renames the DT_RELR tags so that no loader sees them, and a linker script
puts .relr.dyn after .rela.plt, where glibc 2.17's loader needs it. The
executable was tested on glibc 2.17, 2.31 and 2.41, including thread-local
data and a library constructor that calls the program's malloc.

Thread-local data in a library is copied for the threads that exist when
the library is loaded, before it relocates itself. The function fixes the
loading thread's copy; a thread that existed before a dlopen() of the
library keeps a stale copy of the one pointer base has there, so the
library is to be linked, or loaded before threads are started.

On with zebkit_pack_relocations = true (profiles/slim.gn). Startup does the
same work as before, from a smaller table.


